ALiBi (AliBo - a left-right iterative scheme) is an approach for positional encoding introduced by Reimers and Gurevych (2019) for text-dependent speech recognition. ALiBi heavily relies on left-to-right and right-to-left LSTMs and applies an iterative scheme to better capture long-term dependencies in the input sequential data.

To better understand ALiBi positional encoding, let's first familiarize ourselves with the basics of text input for speech recognition tasks. The text input used for speech recognition consists of a sequence of words, or tokens, $$\{t_1, ..., t_N \}$$, where each token has a specific position in the sequence and carries some lexical information.

The goal of positional encoding is to convey the word positions in the input sequence to the model. This can be done by explicitly encoding the position of each token in the sequence or by using self-attention mechanisms that automatically infer position information from the data.

In conventional positional encoding schemes, each position in the sequence is assigned an independent set of learnable embeddings. However, this approach can struggle to capture long-term dependencies in the sequence, since each token will only see its own absolute position encoding.

Alternatively, relative positional encoding schemes assign each token a learnable embedding that encodes the relative position of the current token with respect to other tokens in its local context (e.g., the previous or following tokens). However, this approach may fail to encode necessary global information about the position of the token in the complete sequence.

ALiBi positional encoding combines the advantages of both absolute and relative positional encoding by iteratively updating the positional encoding of each token using information from both its left and right neighbors. The iterative scheme helps ALiBi better capture long-term dependencies in the sequence and significantly outperform conventional positional encoding schemes in text-dependent speech recognition tasks.

In practice, the ALiBi positional encoding is computed as follows:

1. Initialize two zero vectors $$h_0$$ and $$h_{-1}$$. These vectors will be used for left and right positional encodings, respectively.
2. For each token $$t_n$$ in the input sequence, update its left and right positional encodings as follows:

$$
h_n = f_R(h_{n-1}, t_n)
$$

$$
h_{n+1} = f_L(h_n, t_n)
$$

Here, $$f_R$$ and $$f_L$$ represent recurrent functions that are responsible for updating the left and right positional encodings of a token, respectively. These functions can be implemented using LSTMs or other recurrent architectures. Note that, since the first and last tokens in the sequence do not have left and right neighbors, their positional encodings are更新 efter $h_0 = h_{-1} = 0$.

3. Stop the iterative process after a fixed number of iterations $$k$$. This will give you an updated left positional encoding $$h_{n}^{L} = h_n$$ and right positional encoding $$h_{n}^{R} = h_{n-k}$$. These updated positional encodings now capture contextual and positional information from both the left and right neighbors of the token, respectively.
4. Add the updated positional encodings as additional vectors to the token embeddings. The total embedding of each token now consists of its original lexical information and the learnable positional encodings from both the left ($$h_{n}^{L}$$) and right ($$h_{n}^{R}$$) sides.

By iteratively updating the positional encodings in both the left and right directions, ALiBi positional encoding improves the model's ability to capture long-term dependencies and results in better performance for text-dependent speech recognition tasks. The approach can be seamlessly integrated with other architectures, such as Transformers, to further improve their performance in various tasks.
