In the context of generation APIs, stop sequences, also known as stop characters or stop words, are used to define specific patterns or sequences that signal the end of a request or a response. They serve as delimiters to separate different parts of the data and help to ensure the correct interpretation of the API communication.

For instance, in JSON-based APIs, a common stop sequence is the combination of a curly brace `{` and a comma `,`, as these characters usually do not appear inside standard JSON objects. When the API receiving the data encounters this combination, it knows that it has reached the end of an object and can proceed to interpret the next part of the data.

Similarly, in URL queries, an ampersand `&` character is often used as a stop sequence, signaling the end of a single query parameter and the start of another one.

