[97/200] Running: How is top-p sampling different from nucleus sampling?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Top-p sampling and nucleus sampling are both techniques used in sampling during the generation of text by language models. However, they have a different approach in choosing the words to include in the top “k” sampled words.

In Top-p sampling, the model sorts all the possible next words by their probability, and then it samples the top “p” (a hyperparameter) words from the sorted list and continues the text from there. If “p” is less than “k”, the rest of the “k” words are filled with the most probable word.

In Nucleus sampling, the model gathers all possible next words by their probability, sets a threshold for the cumulative probability, and samples all the words that meet or exceed that threshold. The threshold is a fixed percentage of the total probability (another hyperparameter), and the size of the cumulative threshold can be smaller than “k”. The rest of the words are filled with the most probable word to reach the desired “k”.

In short, Top-p sampling selects the top predefined percentage of the most likely words, while Nucleus sampling selects the words that exceed a cumulative threshold representing a percentage of the total probability. Both methods help generate more diverse and unpredictable text compared to using only the most likely word.
