[139/200] Running: What are attention heads and how do they specialize?...

🧠 LLM Response:


Attention heads are a component of the Transformer architecture used in machine learning models for tasks such as language translation and text generation. They allow the model to focus on specific parts of the input sequence at each step, rather than processing all sequences equally.

Each attention head in a Transformer model computes a weighted sum of the input sequences, with the weights determined by a scaled dot product attention mechanism. The weights are based on the query and key-value pairs computed from the input sequences and the output of the previous layer. This allows the model to assign higher weight to important sequences and lower weight to less important sequences.

In multi-head attention, the model processes input sequences through multiple attention heads in parallel, each with its own set of parameters. This allows the model to learn to attend to different sub-sequences or features of the input sequences in different ways.

The attention heads can be thought of as specialized sub-networks that focus on different aspects of the input sequences. For example, one head may specialize in attending to the main subject of a sentence, while another head may specialize in attending to complementary phrases or modifiers. This allows the model to capture more complex patterns in the input data.
