 Just like many fields in science, for assessing software benchmarking the process is just as important as the results. Understanding how data is captured, computed and reported is critical for evaluating and comparing results. Here are suggestions for a more transparent, accurate and reproducible benchmarking process.

### Define the scope of the benchmark

The first step is to focus on benchmarks with well-defined input parameters such that the benchmark being executed produces the expected output consistently. Attempt to create a suite of micro-benchmarks that cover each major part of an application or algorithm. It is often useful to include both extreme cases and more typical scenarios when defining a benchmark suite. Micro-benchmarks should be written such that they are readily comprehensible and reproducible by others. Libraries and tools for benchmarking are available and should be used to the extent possible.

### Document the benchmarking frameworks and tools

Which libraries, tools and benchmarking frameworks were used? For each tool or framework, the version number, build details and any custom code should be documented. It is important that others be able to reproduce not only the benchmark results, but also the methodology used to acquire the results. While this may require additional effort, it is a key component for assessing the reliability and validity of benchmarks. Detailed documentation of the benchmark itself is quite elementary in the academic world and yet many reports do not include enough details. Developers may need to produce software reports without the same level of formality in some contexts, in which case, documenting key aspects of the benchmarking methodology can still be helpful.

### Report details of the experimental platform

Articles reporting the performance of software are essential readings when evaluating tools, making purchasing decisions or assessing the feasibility of a scientific project. The experimental setup always influences the results, sometimes considerably, so it is advisable to report the exact details, or, at the very least the range and types of hardware and software used to conduct the benchmarking.

- Operating system, version and distribution
- Types and configurations of processors and memory, particularly for parallel and distributed applications
- Hardware-related issues such as CPU configurations and specifications
- Network type, topology, latency, throughput and bandwidth
- Details of any libraries, compilers or drivers used
- Configuration details for each of the software being tested, including version, configuration flags, OS, etc.

A list of benchmarked software and their versions should be explicitly stated in the report. In addition, it is relevant to know which benchmarks were a native executables versus those which were interpreters.  For a consistent comparison of results, it is often necessary to use a single programming language and/or compiler for each implementation.

### Precisely define the metrics and units

What is it that will be measured? What operations or functions are considered? How will the results be interpreted? The definition of the metric being assessed is an important part of the benchmarking process. The units of measurement are also critical to the interpretation of the results. For example, if the benchmark measures file throughput, is this measured in bytes per second,MB per second or GB per second? The measurement units will impact the comparison of results.

### What are the input parameters?

Providing details of the input parameters is an essential component of the benchmarking report. In many cases, input parameters can have a profound influence on the observed output. For example, memory allocation and scheduling algorithms are known to impact performance on a real-time operating system. In some sense, measuring performance is closely tied to understanding the specific configuration used. It is important to provide a clear explanation of the input parameters and to carefully document them as part of each benchmark report.

### Using realistic datasets

Input data can have a large impact on benchmarking results. Using modest input sizes can be misleading and artificial constructs may not be useful when assessing an algorithm empirically. In some cases, worst-case and average-case scenarios should be explored. For example, in analyzing sorting algorithms, understanding performance when there are not many runs (small number of distinct values) is also useful for understanding memory utilization characteristics that are present even when limited space.

### Benchmarking with multiple implementations

Comparisons are necessary to quantify the relative performance of different implementations. When developing a benchmark, it is essential to conduct experiments with multiple implementations. In such cases, the goal is to focus on improvements in the particular implementation being assessed, while keeping other factors (such as different hardware) consistent.

