🧠 LLM Response:


In the context of machine learning, a model shard is a segment or a part of a large model that's been divided (or "sharded") for the purpose of distributed computing. This technique is often used when we need to train huge models that don't fit into the memory of a single machine.

So, instead of training the entire model at once, we divide it into smaller parts (shards), each of which can be trained on a different machine. Each machine (or worker) in the training process handles one or more shards, which allows the training process to scale up in parallel, speeding up model training times significantly.

After the training process is complete, the shards are combined to form the full model. This approach is commonly used in deep learning, where models can have hundreds of millions or even billions of parameters.
