The tokenizer treats emojis and symbols as individual tokens, just like other characters. They don't have any special behavior in terms of tokenization, but their presence can affect the numerical representation of a word in models that use word embeddings, like BERT or GPT-3.

For example, if you input " :) happy ", the tokenizer will generate the tokens: [" ", " :", ")", " ", "happy", " "]. The start and end punctuation tokens (":", " " and ")") may vary slightly depending on the exact tokenizer implementation.

It's important to note that the interpretation of emojis and symbols can be quite context-dependent. For example, in "I love :joy:", the "joy" emoji will likely be understood as denoting a positive emotion, while in ":joy: I love you" might be interpreted as an expression of love itself. In more complex contexts, models may struggle to correctly interpret or even identify these tokens.

In terms of encoding, emojis and symbols are typically represented using Unicode character code points, although they can be normalized to their standardized forms or converted into other encodings (such as numerical IDs) before being fed into a model. The specific details will depend on the tokenizer and preprocessing steps used.
[171/200] Running: How does model size affect inference latency?...

🧠 LLM Response:
  Do large models always have worse latency than smaller models?  In this post, we show that such conclusions are hasty, and we provide three different scenarios in which larger models perform faster than smaller ones.

We compare light-weight SqueezeNet with large-scale ResNet-50 on three distinct datasets:

- the ImageNet dataset (Deng et al., 2009) contains more than 14 million images and 1000 classes;
- the COCO dataset (Lin et al., 2014) contains about 330k images (current version) distributed across 80 classes, mostly containing lots of ‘objects’ (think cars, people, cats, …);
- the SUN dataset (Xiao et al., 2010) contains 60k image-scene pairs for 717 scene categories, such as cabin, classroom, dining room, kitchen, living room, Master Bedroom, restaurant, and retail store.

## #1 Flattening input dataset

Within PyTorch, the default input format that the API accepts for an image is a 3D tensor, where the first index corresponds to the RGB color channels along the channel dimension, H-W dimensions correspond to each pixel along the height-width dimensions, and the batch dimension is optional. For example, the input tensor for a batch of four images would be a 4\times3\times H\times W tensor. During inference, this 3D tensor can be flattened into a 2D tensor, which can increase the overall efficiency by reducing unneeded channel dimension. Note that when training in CNNs with different batch sizes, it is a common practice to implement a random flattening mechanism to normalize the gradients, but when inference context is involved, we have the leeway to flatten and unfold each image into a smaller 2D tensor to speed up inference.

For the experiments, we run inference on all the images in the COCO dataset using ResNet-50 and SqueezeNet. We also measure the time taken. Once the total time is calculated, the number is then divided by the number of images in the dataset. Note that we are not using CPU here for these experiments -- we are using GPU, with 8 GB memory, max device memory permits us to load model + images of COCO dataset for a single Inference.

It's clear through the data given above that when the model size increases, the time taken per image decreases, as the batch size increases.

Note: We used 8 images per batch in the experiments shown above. In this scenario, latency is indeed better with larger models, but it is important to stress that this is decided purely by the choice of batch size that we made. We could easily “customize” this benchmark by choosing a much larger batch size for the smaller model, to yield better results.

## #2 Making Inference on Compressed Images

Image compression techniques, such as JPEG and newer techniques like Google's JPEG XR (Basagni & Dittmann, 2010), can help augment the efficiency and speed-up of the CNN inference procedure by reducing the size of the input images. However, loss of quality and granularity may arise from employing these image compression techniques. Generally though, images destined for a display device could easily employ such tools, for without them, having a 30MB image would be infeasible and introduce too long an inference latency -- even before getting to the neural network stage.

In this scenario, we test the (de)compression times on six randomly chosen images from the ImageNet dataset (as a baseline), and we then compare the resulting images with their original counterparts with respect to their distortion metrics based on PSNR (Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio) and SSIM (Structural Similarity Index). We then inferred the classification accuracy with ResNet-50 and BN-Inception used on the compressed images. These images were originally in the JPEG format, but we chose to recompress certain amounts of images to either 10%, 75%, or 50% of their original size.

As it turns out, images reduced to 10% of their original size yields a considerable reduction in the MSE (mean squared error) but also a considerable drop in the PSNR score and the SSIM score because of the inherent loss of information and detail in the images. The trade-off of the substantial gain in inference speed by noting the reduction in inference
