Yes, both options will work, but they address different use cases.

1. Wrapping the `Image` widget inside an `ErrorWidget` is a common pattern when you have a view that depends on multiple streams or data sources, and you want to provide fallback content or handle errors at the view level. This is especially useful when you have complex hierarchies of widgets that rely on the same data source and you don't want to repeat the error handling logic for each widget.

2. Making the stream aware of errors is a more advanced technique that can be useful when you want to handle errors within the stream provider and emit a special state to let the `StreamBuilder` know that an error has occurred. This approach is good when the stream provider is responsible for handling and transforming data from multiple data sources and you want to propagate any errors upstream.

Regarding your question about using your custom stream provider with external libraries, both options should work just fine. However, if you are using a library that already provides a stream provider for the same data source (like `flutter_cache_manager` in your example), it's generally a good idea to use their provided stream provider instead of creating your own. If you still need to handle errors at the view level, you can use the first approach (wrapping the `Image` widget inside an `ErrorWidget`).

Lastly, to check if the stream is in an error state, you can use the `hasError` property of the `AsyncSnapshot` object passed to the `StreamBuilder`'s `builder` parameter. Here's an example:

```
StreamBuilder<UIImage>(
  stream: _myImageStreamProvider,
  builder: (context, snapshot) {
    if (snapshot.hasError) {
      // Handle the error
      return Text('An error occurred: ${snapshot.error}');
    }

    if (snapshot.connectionState == ConnectionState.active) {
      // Show loading indicator
      return CircularProgressIndicator();
    }

    // Show the UIImage
    return Image(image: snapshot.data);
